\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Wavelet plane (time-frequency) showing the relationship between signal slope and required MMNN architecture. Low-slope signals require large width-rank product $W \cdot R$ to achieve time-frequency localization, while high-slope signals require large depth $L$ to grow and capture higher frequency variations. The slope-1 case (e.g., $\cos (x^2)$) represents the critical balanced regime. This demonstrates how the architecture parameters must be tuned to the wavelet structure of the target function.}}{17}{figure.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:wavelet_slope}{{11}{17}{Wavelet plane (time-frequency) showing the relationship between signal slope and required MMNN architecture. Low-slope signals require large width-rank product $W \cdot R$ to achieve time-frequency localization, while high-slope signals require large depth $L$ to grow and capture higher frequency variations. The slope-1 case (e.g., $\cos (x^2)$) represents the critical balanced regime. This demonstrates how the architecture parameters must be tuned to the wavelet structure of the target function}{figure.11}{}}
